narrative:
## Write Contracts First, Code Second

Design by Contract (DbC) inverts the traditional workflow. Instead of coding first and documenting later, you write the contract as the FIRST step:

### Step 1: Write the Signature
```xiom
fn withdraw(account: &mut Account, amount: Float64)
```

### Step 2: Write the Contract
```xiom
fn withdraw(account: &mut Account, amount: Float64)
  requires: amount > 0.0;
  requires: amount <= account.balance;
  ensures: account.balance == account@pre.balance - amount;
```

### Step 3: Now Write the Body
```xiom
fn withdraw(account: &mut Account, amount: Float64)
  requires: amount > 0.0;
  requires: amount <= account.balance;
  ensures: account.balance == account@pre.balance - amount;
{
  account.balance = account.balance - amount;
}
```

### Why This Order Matters

Writing the contract first forces you to answer critical questions BEFORE implementing:
- What must be true before this function runs? (requires)
- What must be true after this function runs? (ensures)
- What can go wrong? (edge cases)

If you can't write the contract, you don't yet understand the function's responsibility. Go back to the design.

### Applied: The Deposit Function
```xiom
fn deposit(account: &mut Account, amount: Float64)
  requires: amount > 0.0;
  ensures: account.balance == account@pre.balance + amount;
{
  account.balance = account.balance + amount;
}
```

The contract is trivially simple — and that's the point. Simple contracts mean clear responsibilities.

